1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:08,144
This is spectral bisection. Sounds fancy, but it is just three steps on a five-node graph.

2
00:00:08,144 --> 00:00:12,532
Give every node a score, sort them, and cut once.

3
00:00:12,532 --> 00:00:14,356
Let's go.

4
00:00:15,206 --> 00:00:22,066
Here is the problem. Five nodes, six edges, and every edge has a weight.

5
00:00:22,066 --> 00:00:29,934
We want two groups. Two rules: do not cut too much weight, and do not let one group get tiny.

6
00:00:29,934 --> 00:00:36,606
Why the second rule? Because the cheap trick is to cut off one node and call it a day.

7
00:00:37,456 --> 00:00:44,724
Look at the numbers. A, B and C are tied together with five, four and one.

8
00:00:44,724 --> 00:00:48,224
D and E are tied with five as well.

9
00:00:48,224 --> 00:00:53,356
Between the two groups there is only a thin bridge: two, and one.

10
00:00:53,356 --> 00:00:57,172
So honestly, you can already see the answer.

11
00:00:58,022 --> 00:01:02,242
Step one: build a table called the Laplacian.

12
00:01:02,242 --> 00:01:09,846
Diagonal: add up the weights on each node. Everywhere else: minus the weight of that edge.

13
00:01:09,846 --> 00:01:13,658
Quick check: every row adds up to zero.

14
00:01:13,658 --> 00:01:23,306
But why build a table at all? Because we want one number that says how bad a split is. And this table can give us that number.

15
00:01:24,156 --> 00:01:32,000
Here is how it gives us that number. Multiply the table by a vector x: one value per node.

16
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:41,476
The answer is a single number. For every edge, take its weight times the squared gap between the two ends, and add them all up.

17
00:01:41,476 --> 00:01:50,476
Big weight with a big gap means a big bill. So this number is small exactly when tightly connected nodes get similar values.

18
00:01:51,326 --> 00:01:58,906
Let's make it concrete. Write a split as plus one on one side, minus one on the other.

19
00:01:58,906 --> 00:02:05,118
Then every edge you cut contributes four, and every edge you keep contributes zero.

20
00:02:05,118 --> 00:02:09,122
So the number is just four times the cut size.

21
00:02:09,122 --> 00:02:17,446
The good split cuts three, so it costs twelve. The bad seed cuts twelve, so it costs forty-eight.

22
00:02:17,446 --> 00:02:21,286
One number, and it already ranks the two splits.

23
00:02:22,136 --> 00:02:27,892
So the real question is: which vector makes this number as small as possible?

24
00:02:27,892 --> 00:02:38,064
Two catches. All-equal gives zero, but that is not a split. And if the length is free, we can shrink everything to zero.

25
00:02:38,064 --> 00:02:43,100
So we fix the length, forbid the flat vector, and minimise.

26
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:49,504
That problem has a standard answer: the eigenvector of the second-smallest eigenvalue.

27
00:02:49,504 --> 00:02:57,592
Eigenvector just means a vector the matrix stretches without turning it. The stretch factor is the eigenvalue.

28
00:02:58,442 --> 00:03:06,190
So step two: take that second-smallest eigenvector. It has a name: the Fiedler vector.

29
00:03:06,190 --> 00:03:14,226
It gives each node a score. Here, E and D come out negative, and C, B, A positive.

30
00:03:14,226 --> 00:03:21,134
It is not exactly plus one and minus one. It is the smooth version of that idea.

31
00:03:21,134 --> 00:03:29,726
But the sign already splits them. And because the values are ordered, we can sort. That is step three.

32
00:03:30,576 --> 00:03:33,884
Step three: sort by score.

33
00:03:33,884 --> 00:03:40,432
E, D, C, B, A. The graph is just a line now.

34
00:03:40,432 --> 00:03:44,032
Four gaps, so four places you could cut.

35
00:03:44,882 --> 00:03:47,182
Try all four.

36
00:03:47,182 --> 00:03:57,186
Cut after E: six. After D: three. After C: five. After B: six.

37
00:03:57,186 --> 00:04:01,866
Keep the cheapest. That is k equals two.

38
00:04:02,716 --> 00:04:09,000
Now look at the middle. k equals three costs five, not three.

39
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,740
Counting nodes tells you nothing about weights.

40
00:04:12,740 --> 00:04:17,584
That is why we check every cut, instead of eyeballing the middle.

41
00:04:17,584 --> 00:04:22,048
Scores propose. The sweep decides.

42
00:04:22,898 --> 00:04:29,278
So: A, B, C on one side. D and E on the other.

43
00:04:29,278 --> 00:04:33,450
The cut is three, and it is only the thin bridge.

44
00:04:33,450 --> 00:04:36,786
Both heavy edges stay inside a group.

45
00:04:37,636 --> 00:04:40,176
Three things to watch.

46
00:04:40,176 --> 00:04:45,716
The score can flip sign. Same split, don't panic.

47
00:04:45,716 --> 00:04:51,880
Disconnected graph? Lambda two is zero, handle it separately.

48
00:04:51,880 --> 00:04:55,548
And if two scores tie, sort stably.

49
00:04:55,548 --> 00:05:00,852
Now go run it yourself. Next up: Kernighan Lin.

